---
title: "Row Dragging"
---

Row dragging is used to rearrange rows by dragging the row with the mouse. To
enable row dragging, set the column property `rowDrag` on one (typically
the first) column.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Row Drag Simple Managed" name="simple-managed" /%}

The example above shows [Managed Row Dragging](./row-dragging-managed), enabled by setting `rowDrag` on the column and `rowDragManaged` on the grid options.

## Enabling Row Dragging

{% apiDocumentation source="column-properties/properties.json" section="row dragging" names=["rowDrag"] /%}

To enable row dragging on all rows, set the column property `rowDrag = true` on one (typically the first) column and configure managed or unmanaged row dragging.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        // make all rows draggable
        { field: 'athlete', rowDrag: true },
    ],
}
```

The callback form of `rowDrag` is deprecated. To control which rows can be dragged, use [isRowDraggable](#preventing-rows-being-dragged) instead.

There are two ways in which row dragging works in the grid, managed and unmanaged:

- **[Managed Row Dragging](./row-dragging-managed)**: This is the simplest, where the grid will rearrange rows as you drag them.
- **[Unmanaged Row Dragging](./row-dragging-unmanaged)**: This is more complex and more powerful. The grid will not rearrange rows as you drag. Instead the application is responsible for responding to events fired by the grid and rows are rearranged by the application.

See also:

- [Row Dragging with Tree Data](./tree-data-row-dragging), both for managed and unmanaged row dragging with [Tree Data](./tree-data/).
- [Row Dragging Between Grids](./row-dragging-to-grid/) to drag rows between two grids.
- [Row Dragging to an External DropZone](./row-dragging-to-external-dropzone/) to drag rows from the grid to an external drop zone.

## Preventing Rows Being Dragged

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-options/properties.json" section="rowDragging" names=["isRowDraggable"] /%}

Use the [isRowDraggable](./grid-options/#reference-rowDragging-isRowDraggable) grid option to decide, row by row, whether a drag can start on a row. It applies to every way of starting a drag: the drag handle of any column with `rowDrag: true`, and the entire row when `rowDragEntireRow` is enabled.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'athlete', rowDrag: true },
        { field: 'locked' },
    ],
    rowDragManaged: true,
    isRowDraggable: params => !params.data?.locked,
}
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Preventing Rows Being Dragged" name="is-row-draggable" /%}

In the example above, rows with the **Locked** box ticked cannot be dragged. Note the following:

- The drag handle is hidden on locked rows, but keeps its space so the column stays aligned.
- Ticking or unticking **Locked** updates the row data, so the handle and the row's draggability change straight away.
- **Drag Entire Row** enables `rowDragEntireRow`. Locked rows then cannot be dragged from any cell.
- Other rows can still be dropped above or below a locked row.

The `data` property of the params is `undefined` for group and footer rows, so use `node` to decide for those rows.

### Interaction with the rowDrag Column Property

The `rowDrag` column property decides whether a column has a drag handle. `isRowDraggable` decides whether a row can be dragged from it.

| `rowDrag` | `isRowDraggable` returns | Result in that column                                                       |
| --------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `false`   | any value                | No handle is shown and no space is reserved for one.                        |
| `true`    | `false`                  | The handle is hidden, but its space is reserved. The row cannot be dragged. |
| `true`    | `true`                   | The handle is shown and the row can be dragged.                             |

With a `rowDrag: false` column, whether the row can be dragged from elsewhere still depends on `isRowDraggable` and the other drag sources.

### Notes

- With [Multi-Row Dragging](./row-dragging-managed/#multi-row-dragging) (`rowDragMultiRow`), rows for which `isRowDraggable` returns `false` are left out of the drag, whether it starts from a handle or from the entire row. A drag cannot start on such a row.
- `isRowDraggable` is re-evaluated when the row data changes. If the callback reads state from outside the row data, update the row data, call `api.redrawRows()` or set `isRowDraggable` to a new callback for the change to take effect. Setting the same callback again has no effect.
- `isRowDraggable` only controls whether a row can be picked up. Other rows can still be dropped around it, which can change its position. To control where rows can be dropped, use [isRowValidDropPosition](./row-dragging-managed/#preventing-dropping-on-certain-rows).
- It works with both managed and unmanaged row dragging. No row drag events fire for a row whose drag was prevented.
- When `isRowDraggable` is set, the result of a `rowDrag` column property callback is ignored.
- A custom drag component registered with `registerRowDragger` cannot start a drag on a row that is not draggable, but whether it is visible is left to the application. See [Custom Row Dragger](./row-dragging-customisation/#row-dragger-inside-custom-cell-renderers).
